#Game State

Everything in the game that moves, or is eaten, is a marking of the one net:
where the chompers are, where the ghosts are, and where the dots still are.
The game state is those three markings, the score, and the number of lives
remaining. There is no other kind of thing in it; a chomper is not an object
with a position, it is a token count on a place.

The three differ only in the rule that changes them. A chomper marking changes
when the player fires a transition, a ghost marking when a ghost turn does, and
a dot marking never moves a token at all: a dot is consumed when a chomper is
in its place, and is not put back when a life is lost. Keeping them as
separate markings keeps the net semantics clean — each population fires
independently — and makes collisions easy to see, since a place where a
chomper marking and a ghost marking both have tokens is a collision.

A map's chomper start may hold tokens on several places, or several on one,
and all of them are restored on a life reset. A single token is the common
case; several expose fork-join coordination from the very first move.

    interface GameState {
      gn: GameNet;
      chompers: Marking;     // player's tokens
      ghosts: Marking;       // ghost tokens
      dots: Marking;         // dots still to eat, per place
      score: number;
      lives: number;
    }

Each of the three starts as a copy of the map's marking for it: chomperStart,
ghostStart and dotStart from the #Game Map. On a post-death reset the chompers
and the ghosts are dealt again from the same markings, and the dots are not,
unless the map's keepOnReset says otherwise: it names the colours a reset
leaves alone, and by default that is the dots alone.

    function spawnChompers(gn: GameNet): Marking {
      return new Map(gn.chomperStart);
    }

    function spawnGhosts(gn: GameNet): Marking {
      return new Map(gn.ghostStart);
    }

    function initState(gn: GameNet, lives = 3): GameState {
      return {
        gn,
        chompers: spawnChompers(gn),
        ghosts: spawnGhosts(gn),
        dots: new Map(gn.dotStart),
        score: 0,
        lives,
      };
    }

##Dot Eating and Scoring

When a chomper is in a place that has dots, every dot there is removed and the
score rises by ten for each. This happens as a side-effect of firing a
transition — the arrival place is checked after each fire.

    function eatDots(state: GameState): void {
      for (const [placeId, count] of state.chompers) {
        const here = tokenCount(state.dots, placeId);
        if (count > 0 && here > 0) {
          markingRemoveTokens(state.dots, placeId, here);
          state.score += 10 * here;
        }
      }
    }

##Collision Detection

Chompers, ghosts and dots are three token colours in the same net — a coloured
Petri net in all but name. A collision is the chomper and ghost colours
sharing a place. Rather than a global reset, the ghost consumes exactly one
chomper token from each shared place. Only when no chomper tokens remain does
the player lose a life, and the markings the map does not keep are dealt
again — by default the chompers and ghosts, not the dots.

This makes splitting tactically meaningful: two chompers grant resilience, as
a ghost can only eat one per encounter. It also keeps the token semantics
consistent — removal is always a consumption event.

resolveCollisions returns the place ids where a chomper was just eaten, so
the renderer can flash the spot — the state mutation and the information a
caller needs to animate it happen together, in the one place that knows it.

resetToStart and loseLife are shared with the deadlock check below — losing
a life always means "reset positions," whether a ghost ate your last token
or the net you're standing on simply has nothing left to fire.

    function hasCollision(state: GameState): boolean {
      for (const [placeId, count] of state.chompers) {
        if (count > 0 && (state.ghosts.get(placeId) ?? 0) > 0)
          return true;
      }
      return false;
    }

    function keptOnReset(gn: GameNet): ColourName[] {
      return gn.keepOnReset ?? ['dot'];
    }

    function resetToStart(state: GameState): void {
      const keep = keptOnReset(state.gn);
      if (!keep.includes('chomper')) state.chompers = spawnChompers(state.gn);
      if (!keep.includes('ghost'))   state.ghosts = spawnGhosts(state.gn);
      if (!keep.includes('dot'))     state.dots = new Map(state.gn.dotStart);
    }

    function loseLife(state: GameState): void {
      state.lives -= 1;
      resetToStart(state);
    }

    function resolveCollisions(state: GameState): string[] {
      const eaten: string[] = [];
      for (const [placeId, count] of state.chompers) {
        if (count > 0 && (state.ghosts.get(placeId) ?? 0) > 0)
          eaten.push(placeId);
      }
      if (eaten.length === 0) return eaten;
      for (const placeId of eaten)
        markingRemoveTokens(state.chompers, placeId, 1);
      const total = [...state.chompers.values()].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
      if (total > 0) return eaten;
      loseLife(state);
      return eaten;
    }

##Deadlock

A deadlock is a Petri-net concept in its own right: a marking with tokens
still in it, but no transition anywhere enabled for any of them. On a map
like Mandala, where a hard two-token join has no slack, losing one half of
a pair can strand the other half forever with nowhere left to fire — exactly
the condition the escape routes exist to prevent, but a design can still
produce one, and a player should never be stuck looking at a frozen board
with no explanation. Treating it the same as a fatal collision — lose a
life, reset to start — keeps it a recoverable setback rather than a silent
hang, and teaches the concept the same way collisions teach "the ghost ate
you": by naming the Petri-net event that just happened.

    function isDeadlocked(state: GameState): boolean {
      if (markingTotal(state.chompers) === 0) return false;
      return enabledTransitions(state.gn.net, state.chompers).length === 0;
    }

##Win and Lose Conditions

The player wins when the dot marking is empty. The player loses when lives
reach zero.

    function isWon(state: GameState): boolean {
      return markingTotal(state.dots) === 0;
    }

    function isLost(state: GameState): boolean {
      return state.lives <= 0;
    }

The examples below use the circuit map as a representative multi-place net.

    const _crossGn = makeCircuitMap();
    const _s0 = initState(_crossGn);

    function _testEatDot(): { dotsGone: boolean; score: number } {
      const st = initState(_crossGn);
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'nw', 1);
      markingRemoveTokens(st.chompers, 'depot', 1);
      eatDots(st);
      return { dotsGone: tokenCount(st.dots, 'nw') === 0, score: st.score };
    }

    function _testCollision(): boolean {
      const sc = initState(_crossGn);
      markingAddTokens(sc.ghosts, 'depot', 1);
      return hasCollision(sc);
    }

    function _testEatOneChomper(): { upperGone: boolean; lowerSurvived: boolean; livesUnchanged: boolean } {
      const st = initState(makeSplitMap());
      st.chompers.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'upper', 1);
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'lower', 1);
      st.ghosts.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.ghosts, 'upper', 1);
      resolveCollisions(st);
      return {
        upperGone:      tokenCount(st.chompers, 'upper') === 0,
        lowerSurvived:  tokenCount(st.chompers, 'lower') === 1,
        livesUnchanged: st.lives === 3,
      };
    }

    function _testLastChomperEaten(): { livesLost: boolean; resetToStart: boolean } {
      const st = initState(_crossGn);
      st.ghosts.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.ghosts, 'depot', 1);
      resolveCollisions(st);
      return {
        livesLost:    st.lives === 2,
        resetToStart: tokenCount(st.chompers, 'depot') === 1,
      };
    }

A lone token at the Mandala map's west collector is a reachable deadlock:
t_join_w produces it by consuming both nw_c and sw_c, so getting there means
nothing is left on the west arm to retreat to — and t_join needs a token in
c_e too, which this scenario doesn't have. c_w has no escape route of its
own, unlike the corridor places.

    function _testDeadlock(): boolean {
      const st = initState(makeMandalaMap());
      st.chompers.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'c_w', 1);
      return isDeadlocked(st);
    }

    function _testDeadlockRecovery(): {
      livesLost: boolean; resetToStart: boolean;
    } {
      const st = initState(makeMandalaMap());
      st.chompers.clear();
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'c_w', 1);
      loseLife(st);
      return {
        livesLost:    st.lives === 2,
        resetToStart: tokenCount(st.chompers, 'hub') === 1,
      };
    }

A reset after one dot has been eaten and both the chomper and the ghost have
wandered off, to see which of the three markings a map's keepOnReset leaves
alone: the dot comes back or not, and the chomper and ghost stay or go home.

    function _resetOutcome(keep?: ColourName[]): {
      dotBack: boolean; chomperHome: boolean; ghostHome: boolean;
    } {
      const gn: GameNet = { ...makeCircuitMap(), keepOnReset: keep };
      const st = initState(gn);
      st.dots.set('nw', 0);
      st.chompers = emptyMarking();
      markingAddTokens(st.chompers, 'nw', 1);
      st.ghosts = emptyMarking();
      markingAddTokens(st.ghosts, 'nn', 1);
      resetToStart(st);
      return {
        dotBack:     tokenCount(st.dots, 'nw') === 1,
        chomperHome: tokenCount(st.chompers, 'depot') === 1,
        ghostHome:   tokenCount(st.ghosts, 'east') === 1,
      };
    }

##Properties of the markings

The examples above fix particular cases. What must hold for any marking is
stated as properties, over the built-in maps and arbitrary counts.

Two markings are the same when every place holds the same number of tokens,
whether or not one of them has a zero entry the other lacks:

    function _sameTokens(a: Marking, b: Marking): boolean {
      const ids = new Set([...a.keys(), ...b.keys()]);
      return [...ids].every(id => tokenCount(a, id) === tokenCount(b, id));
    }

    const _propMaps = [makeCircuitMap, makeMandalaMap, makeSplitMap, makeDiamondMap];

A state is built over a map, with dots and chompers placed from an arbitrary
list of per-place counts, taken in the order the map's places were declared
and wrapping round when the list is shorter than the places:

    function _stateWith(
      make: () => GameNet,
      cells: Array<{ chomper: boolean; dots: number; ghost: boolean }>,
    ): GameState {
      const st = initState(make());
      st.dots = emptyMarking();
      st.chompers = emptyMarking();
      st.ghosts = emptyMarking();
      [...st.gn.net.places.keys()].forEach((id, i) => {
        const c = cells[i % cells.length];
        st.dots.set(id, c.dots);                      // zeros kept on purpose
        if (c.chomper) markingAddTokens(st.chompers, id, 1);
        if (c.ghost)   markingAddTokens(st.ghosts, id, 1);
      });
      return st;
    }

The generator of those counts is built from fast-check's own constructors,
which exist only inside a property, so each property passes them in:

    function _cellArb(fcLib: any): any {
      return fcLib.array(
        fcLib.record({
          chomper: fcLib.boolean(),
          dots:    fcLib.integer({ min: 0, max: 3 }),
          ghost:   fcLib.boolean(),
        }),
        { minLength: 1, maxLength: 14 },
      );
    }

Eating conserves dots and pays for them. Whatever the counts, eatDots empties
exactly the places that hold a chomper, raises the score by ten for each dot
it took, and touches nothing else:

~property eating_conserves_dots_and_pays_ten_each
    fc.assert(fc.property(
      fc.constantFrom(..._propMaps), _cellArb(fc),
      (make, cells) => {
        const st = _stateWith(make, cells);
        const before = new Map(st.dots);
        const eaten = [...before.entries()]
          .filter(([id]) => tokenCount(st.chompers, id) > 0)
          .reduce((n, [, c]) => n + c, 0);
        eatDots(st);
        const untouched = [...before.entries()]
          .filter(([id]) => tokenCount(st.chompers, id) === 0)
          .every(([id, c]) => tokenCount(st.dots, id) === c);
        const emptied = [...before.keys()]
          .filter(id => tokenCount(st.chompers, id) > 0)
          .every(id => tokenCount(st.dots, id) === 0);
        return markingTotal(before) === markingTotal(st.dots) + eaten &&
               st.score === 10 * eaten &&
               [...st.dots.values()].every(n => n >= 0) &&
               untouched && emptied;
      }
    ))

The game is won exactly when the dot marking holds nothing, including when
places that were eaten from sit in it at zero:

~property won_iff_no_dots_remain
    fc.assert(fc.property(
      fc.constantFrom(..._propMaps), _cellArb(fc),
      (make, cells) => {
        const st = _stateWith(make, cells);
        return isWon(st) === (markingTotal(st.dots) === 0);
      }
    ))

A reset deals again exactly the colours the map does not keep, from the map's
own start markings, and leaves the kept ones as they were. Dealing twice is
the same as dealing once:

~property reset_deals_what_is_not_kept
    fc.assert(fc.property(
      fc.constantFrom(..._propMaps), _cellArb(fc),
      fc.subarray(['chomper', 'ghost', 'dot'] as ColourName[]),
      (make, cells, keep) => {
        const st = _stateWith(make, cells);
        st.gn = { ...st.gn, keepOnReset: keep };
        const was = {
          chomper: new Map(st.chompers), ghost: new Map(st.ghosts),
          dot: new Map(st.dots),
        };
        resetToStart(st);
        const once = {
          chomper: new Map(st.chompers), ghost: new Map(st.ghosts),
          dot: new Map(st.dots),
        };
        resetToStart(st);
        const start = {
          chomper: st.gn.chomperStart, ghost: st.gn.ghostStart,
          dot: st.gn.dotStart,
        };
        const colours: ColourName[] = ['chomper', 'ghost', 'dot'];
        const dealtRight = colours.every(c =>
          _sameTokens(once[c], keep.includes(c) ? was[c] : start[c]));
        const idempotent =
          _sameTokens(once.chomper, st.chompers) &&
          _sameTokens(once.ghost, st.ghosts) &&
          _sameTokens(once.dot, st.dots);
        return dealtRight && idempotent;
      }
    ))

~example
    tokenCount(_s0.chompers, 'depot') === 1
    !isDeadlocked(_s0)
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'nw') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'nn') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'ne') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'sw') === 1
    tokenCount(_s0.dots, 'depot') === 0
    _s0.score === 0
    _s0.lives === 3
    !isWon(_s0)
    !isLost(_s0)

---

#Tests

##eating a dot increases score and removes dot
    _testEatDot().dotsGone === true
    _testEatDot().score === 10

##collision detected when chomper and ghost share a place
    _testCollision() === true

##no collision when they differ
    hasCollision(_s0) === false

##split: ghost eats one chomper, other survives, no life lost
    _testEatOneChomper().upperGone
    _testEatOneChomper().lowerSurvived
    _testEatOneChomper().livesUnchanged

##last chomper eaten loses a life and resets to start
    _testLastChomperEaten().livesLost
    _testLastChomperEaten().resetToStart

##a lone token at the west collector is deadlocked
    _testDeadlock()

##deadlock recovery loses a life and resets to start
    _testDeadlockRecovery().livesLost
    _testDeadlockRecovery().resetToStart

##by default a reset keeps the dots and deals the chompers and ghosts again
    !_resetOutcome().dotBack
    _resetOutcome().chomperHome
    _resetOutcome().ghostHome

##a map that keeps nothing gets its dots back too
    _resetOutcome([]).dotBack
    _resetOutcome([]).chomperHome

##a map can keep the ghosts where they were
    !_resetOutcome(['dot', 'ghost']).ghostHome
    _resetOutcome(['dot', 'ghost']).chomperHome

